{-
   ==========================================================================================
   `Process` - spawning, waiting, pipes and signals - Design-Test.md §11.2's F4.
   ==========================================================================================

   What a harness is made of. §11.2's row says it plainly - "no spawn, no wait, no pipe, no `kill`" -
   and every one of those is what stands between the runner of §4 and the harness of §6: sharding,
   `-isolate`, crash attribution and timeouts are all one process watching another.

   **`.native.yana`, so this module does not exist on JavaScript**, which is §11.2's own ruling:
   `Test.Harness` is native, the JavaScript target gets the *runner* - in-process cases, sections,
   the report stream through `console` - and orchestration happens in a native parent that spawns
   `node`. Saying that outright is better than a `Process` module that is half a lie on one target.

   **The one rule everything here obeys**: between a fork and an exec, a child may do only what is
   async-signal-safe. It may not allocate, and it may not run a teardown - the heap it shares with
   its parent is a copy of a heap some other thread of the parent may have been in the middle of.
   So every allocation a child needs is made *before* the fork, in the parent, and `spawnWith` below
   is the one place that sequence is written.
-}

import Native
import File

{-
   A child, by identifier.

   Not `Drop`, deliberately. A process is not storage and dropping the handle must not do anything to
   it: a parent that goes out of scope without waiting leaves a zombie, which is a fact about the
   program and one the type system has no business hiding by reaping it at a scope end. `waitFor` is
   the only thing that reaps, and forgetting it is visible in `ps` rather than silently repaired.
-}
pub data Process {id: Size}

-- Which end is which. A pipe is two descriptors and their names are the whole of what a caller has to
-- get right, so they are named rather than positional.
pub data Pipe {readEnd: File, writeEnd: File}

{-
   How a child's three standard descriptors are set up.

   `Nothing` means "the parent's own", which is what a test runner wants for a case it is not
   capturing: the child writes straight to the terminal and nothing has to be pumped. A `File` means
   that descriptor, duplicated onto 0, 1 or 2 in the child - which is what the write end of a pipe is
   for.

   Three fields rather than an enum per descriptor, because the three are independent: capturing
   output while leaving errors on the terminal is an ordinary thing to want.

   **And a fourth, which is descriptor 3 and is not standard.** Design-Test.md §5.2 puts a test
   runner's report stream on 3 exactly so that it is not on the same descriptor as what the program
   under test prints, and the only place a child's 3 can be set is here - in the four calls between
   the fork and the exec. Naming it `report` rather than `extra` says what the number is for: a
   general "any descriptor" facility would be a list and a loop in the child, which is more code in
   the one place where nothing may allocate.
-}
pub data Redirect {input: Maybe(File), output: Maybe(File), errors: Maybe(File), report: Maybe(File)}

pub fn inherited() -> Redirect =
    Redirect {input: Nothing, output: Nothing, errors: Nothing, report: Nothing}

{-
   What a finished child answered.

   The two cases the kernel's status word distinguishes, kept apart rather than folded into one
   number - which is `YanaElfTest`'s constraint said in a type. A process exit status carries eight
   bits and a signal is not one of them, so "exited with 134" and "was killed by SIGABRT" are
   different facts that the shell convention of adding 128 conflates.
-}
pub data Exit = Exited(Int) | Signalled(Int)

{-
   Starting one.

   The sequence, and every allocation in it is the parent's:

     1. the path and each argument are copied with a NUL appended;
     2. the vector of pointers into those copies is built, NUL-terminated;
     3. `forkProcess`;
     4. in the child - and only these four calls - the redirections are duplicated into place, the
        exec happens, and a failed exec exits rather than returning into a copy of the parent's
        program;
     5. in the parent, the identifier.

   `arguments` is what the child sees *after* its own name: the vector's first entry is the path, on
   the C convention, and a caller that wanted to lie about `argv[0]` has no way to here. That is
   deliberate - the one use for it is disguising a process, and a test harness has none.
-}
{-
   **A descriptor in a `Redirect` does not keep its file alive**, and a caller has to.

   `Just(borrowFile(f.handle))` is how one of these is filled, and it goes through a number: the
   record holds a `File` that owns nothing, so nothing tells the ownership pass that `f` has to
   outlive the spawn. A value whose last use has gone past is dropped there rather than at the end of
   its scope, and a dropped `File` is a closed descriptor - so an `f` whose last mention is the
   `.handle` inside this record is *already shut* by the time the child duplicates it, and the child
   silently gets the parent's own descriptor instead. Mention the owner after the spawn - closing it
   is the natural thing to do with it - and the window closes with it.
-}
pub fn spawn(path: String, arguments: [String]) -> Result(FileError, Process) =
    spawnWith(path, arguments, inherited())

pub fn spawnWith(path: String, arguments: [String], into: Redirect) -> Result(FileError, Process):
    {-
       Every string the child will read, terminated, and kept alive until after the exec.

       `terminated` is `File.yana`'s rule applied here: a kernel string is NUL-terminated bytes and a
       `String` is a run and a live prefix, so what the kernel gets is a copy. The copies live in
       `held`, whose extent covers the fork - which is what makes the pointers below point at
       something.
    -}
    let &held = [terminated(path)] :: [String]
    let &i = 0 :: Size

    while i < length(arguments):
        push(held, terminated(arguments[i]))
        i = i + 1

    -- The vector itself: one pointer per entry and a null to end it, which is what `execve` reads.
    let &vector = [] :: [%U8]
    let &j = 0 :: Size

    while j < length(held):
        push(vector, bytesOf(held[j]))
        j = j + 1

    push(vector, null() :: %U8)

    let child = forkProcess()
    if child < 0 then return Err(errorOf(0 - child))

    if child == 0:
        -- The child, and only what is safe here. Nothing below allocates, nothing runs a teardown,
        -- and the last line does not come back.
        redirectTo(into.input, 0)
        redirectTo(into.output, 1)
        redirectTo(into.errors, 2)
        redirectTo(into.report, 3)

        execProcess(bytesOf(held[0]), vectorBase(vector), commandLineEnvironment)

        -- Only reachable when the exec failed. 127 is what a shell reports for a command it could
        -- not run, which is the number a parent already knows how to read.
        exitProcess(127)

    return Ok(Process {id: child})

-- One descriptor put in place, or left alone. `Nothing` is the parent's own, inherited across the
-- fork with nothing to do.
fn redirectTo(file: Maybe(File), to: Size) -> {} = match file:
    Just(file) -> duplicateHandle(file.handle, to)
    Nothing -> {}

{-
   Waiting for one, and what it answered.

   Blocking. A timeout is `signal` plus a second wait, and it is the harness's to compose rather than
   a parameter here: what a runner does when a case hangs - kill it, attribute the case, go on - is a
   policy, and a `waitFor(timeout)` that returned `Nothing` would be that policy with the reporting
   left out.
-}
pub fn waitFor(self: Process) -> Result(FileError, Exit):
    let &status = 0 :: Int
    let answer = waitProcess(self.id, addressOf(status), 0)

    if answer < 0 then return Err(errorOf(0 - answer))
    return Ok(decodeStatus(status))

{-
   Whether it has finished, without blocking.

   `Nothing` is "still running", which is what `WNOHANG` answers with a zero identifier. The
   distinction between that and an error is the whole reason this is not `waitFor` with a flag.
-}
pub fn finished(self: Process) -> Result(FileError, Maybe(Exit)):
    let &status = 0 :: Int
    let answer = waitProcess(self.id, addressOf(status), waitNonBlocking)

    if answer < 0 then return Err(errorOf(0 - answer))
    if answer == 0 then return Ok(Nothing)

    return Ok(Just(decodeStatus(status)))

{-
   The kernel's status word, decoded - the one piece of bit-twiddling in this file.

   The low seven bits are the terminating signal and are zero for an ordinary exit; the next eight
   are the status a process reported. That is the layout `waitpid` has always had, and the reason it
   is written here rather than in the platform file is the line that file draws: there is the
   kernel's number and the kernel's answer, and every interpretation of one is somewhere else.
-}
fn decodeStatus(status: Int) -> Exit:
    let signal = status `and` 127
    if signal != 0 then return Signalled(signal)

    return Exited((status `shr` 8) `and` 255)

-- A signal, by number. What a timeout sends, and the reason `kill` is in F4's list at all: a case
-- that hangs has to be stopped and attributed rather than taking the run down with it.
pub fn signal(self: Process, number: Int) -> Result(FileError, {}) =
    checkedUnit(signalProcess(self.id, number))

-- A kernel answer that carries nothing but whether it worked, as a `Result`. `File.yana` has the
-- same three lines and they stay separate: what a module reports out of a raw answer is the module's,
-- and sharing one would put `Process`'s failures into `File`'s vocabulary by declaration rather than
-- by decision.
fn checkedUnit(answer: Size) -> Result(FileError, {}):
    if answer < 0 then return Err(errorOf(0 - answer))
    return Ok({})

pub let terminateSignal = 15 :: Int
pub let killSignal = 9 :: Int

{-
   A pipe, as two `File`s that own their ends.

   Owning is the point: a parent that captures a child's output has to close the *write* end after
   the fork or the read never sees an end of file, and a `File`'s own teardown is what makes that a
   scope rather than a rule to remember.
-}
pub fn newPipe() -> Result(FileError, Pipe):
    let &ends = [0, 0] :: [Int]
    let answer = makePipe(intBase(ends))

    if answer < 0 then return Err(errorOf(0 - answer))
    return Ok(Pipe {readEnd: adoptFile(ends[0] :: Size), writeEnd: adoptFile(ends[1] :: Size)})

{-
   ==========================================================================================
   The three pointer helpers, which are the only place this file reaches past a value.
   ==========================================================================================

   Each takes its subject with `return`, which is `File.yana`'s rule and is the whole reason that
   file has no layer of one-line syscall wrappers: a raw pointer carries provenance exactly as a
   borrow does, and an undeclared signature is accepted in silence and then releases what the pointer
   points at before the call that reads through it. The marker is what places the drop after the call.
-}
fn bytesOf(return text: String) -> %U8 = bitcast(values(stringData(text).bytes)) :: %U8

fn vectorBase(return items: [%U8]) -> Ptr(Ptr(U8)) = bitcast(values(items)) :: Ptr(Ptr(U8))

fn intBase(return items: [Int]) -> %Int = values(items)

fn terminated(text: String) -> String:
    let &copy = String.ofCapacity(length(text) + 1)
    String.push(copy, text)
    String.pushUnit(copy, 0)

    return copy
